Z||000003||fierose||07-01-2000||09:40 AM||||Well put||||reg||6
Z||000004||BN Boomer||07-01-2000||10:07 AM||||Let's start by comparing apples to apples, here. Try buying a Civic the same age as a Fiero and see if you have any less problems or if those cars are any easier to work on when they do have problems. My 84 Civic had the timing gear snap off of the camshaft, apparently not an uncommon problem due to a poor design, and it was all downhill from there. They are are a total pain in the arse to work on, parts are hard to find and expensive, and after all that frustration you are still just left with a crappy old Civic. I wound up practically just giving the thing away as it became increasingly apparent that the investment of money and time required to keep it going wasn't going to be worth anywhere near the outcome. It was a decent enough car while it lasted and did give us years of good service, but it just another bland Japanese cookie cutter car and sure wasn't anything like my 85 GT. <P>I would <B>much</B> rather have a Fiero that is something you can be proud of and worthy of the effort to keep alive. They are truly a labor of love. I've only been into Fieros for a year now and I was absolutely amazed, stunned, at the support there is for the Fiero on the internet. I swear the base of knowledge and technical expertise available in the Fiero community is second to none among other various types of automobiles. <P>I doubt even the newer Civics will have as much staying power as the Fiero enjoys. 15 years from now the ones that are even left will be about as cool as a 15 year old one is today, which, by the way, ain't too much.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>As for your problems...<P>1. As has already been suggested, take the seat cover off.<P>2. Given that the back wheels don't turn to steer like the front ones do, would the CV joints even clatter like typical FWD ones if they were bad when turning a corner? I'd look into the motor mount thing, too.<P>3. What kind of alternators are you using? Parts house cheapies? If so, start by getting one of the lifetime warranty ones. Do you have electrical accessories that draw a lot of power? <P><p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 07-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spyder||07-01-2000||11:09 AM||||Here is a link on how to replace the head rest speakers:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mrmikes.com/faq.htm#getspeakers" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mrmikes.com/faq.htm#getspeakers</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||shain||07-02-2000||04:51 AM||||no need to get defensive! i dont WANT a jap mobile!! its just that these crappy citation 4 bangers suck! i want/need a v6 gt! geeezz! any ways the"knocking" was just a wheel bearing which i easily replaced.  <BR>thakns for the info on the speakers <BR>and i am running a 400 watt amp+sub,so i guess that is killing my alternators. <P>i suppose i need a fatter amp like 100 or somthing if i will continue to run my system<P>thanx again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000007||Oreif||07-02-2000||07:30 AM||||If you have a large stereo system that is overheating your alternator, The easiest way is to remove your alternator and find a shop that can rewind the alternator. By using a slightly thinner wire with more windings you can have the alternator wound to 135 amps.<BR>Alternator rewinds cost around $80.00. It's cheaper than replacing your alternator every time you cook it. (Also helps remove noise from large stereo systems)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||07-02-2000||10:00 AM||||Make sure you have a really good capacitor on the amp too, if it isn't handling all the draw, that can be hard on the alternator too.  <P>Taiji<BR>modified 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fret Naught||07-03-2000||01:08 AM||||I had a civic once, NEVER AGAIN!!!!!!!!!!!!<BR>On the alternator, I know re-winding will fix the problem, but I wonder if you couldn't just buy a higher rated one as opposed to re-winding.||||reg||4
